content:
{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: all six findings are low severity, and most look like normal setup for a home PC. The one thing worth checking first is the second administrator account, <computer>\\<account-2>. Make sure you know who created it. After that, consider the advice about not using an administrator account for everyday work. The startup apps and the HP dock entry look like ordinary software.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "A second administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The computer has another account, <computer>\\<account-2>, with full administrator rights. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice. But an admin account you don't recognize is a common sign that someone has broken in. If you or someone you trust set it up, this is fine. If not, look at it more closely. The report shows what to do for this finding."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Your everyday account has administrator rights",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-DAILY-ADMIN:44c65344"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account you normally use, <computer>\\<user>, is an administrator. This is very common on personal computers. The downside is that anything you run, including something harmful you open by mistake, can gain full control of the computer once you click through a UAC prompt. Using a standard account day to day lowers that risk. The suggested fix is in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Apps that start when you sign in (Discord, Spotify, Notion)",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:46fde10b",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:a6556492",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:422bd707"
      ],
      "explanation": "Three programs start automatically when you sign in: Discord, Spotify and Notion. Each runs from inside your user folder. They were flagged because other programs can also write to that folder. Many normal apps, these three included, install themselves this way. If you use these apps, this is expected and nothing to worry about. If you don't recognize one, check it using the details in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "HP dock firmware tool set to run on a trigger",
      "finding_ids": [
        "WMI-CONSUMER-REVIEW:7d52aadf"
      ],
      "explanation": "A background Windows trigger (called a WMI event subscription) is set up to run HP's firmware installer for the HP USB-C Dock G5. The program sits in Program Files, a folder only administrators can write to, and the scan found nothing suspicious about it. Dock firmware updaters often work this way. If you use an HP USB-C dock, this is almost certainly normal."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
